# Sensor tuning — suppressions for this deployment.
#
# These are Suricata *engine* events, not detections of anything. They fire
# because the sensor watches veth interfaces inside Docker, where the peer
# end of each pair still coalesces segments: Suricata is handed frames whose
# captured length disagrees with their claimed length, and says so — tens of
# thousands of times during a large file transfer.
#
# Leaving them on would bury every real alert, which is the classic way a
# sensor becomes useless: not by missing things, but by shouting constantly.
# Tuning out known-benign engine noise is the first thing you do on a real
# deployment, and you document why — that is what this file is.
#
# None of these suppress an attack signature. Attack rules are untouched.

# 2200122 AF-PACKET truncated packet
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200122
# 2200003 IPv4 truncated packet
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200003
# 2210044 STREAM packet with invalid timestamp
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210044
# 2200074 TCPv4 invalid checksum
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200074
# 2200075 UDPv4 invalid checksum
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200075
# 2210045 STREAM packet with broken ack
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210045
# 2210029 STREAM 3way handshake wrong seq
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210029
# 2210054 STREAM excessive retransmissions
# The WAN links in this lab lose packets on purpose (the satellite link
# always, the Lab 03 faults on demand). Retransmissions are the expected
# consequence, not a finding.
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2210054
